n-49","fingerprint":"fb43693c75ab966bd26d25dc60bf5bdb39f549405b4163877817d053029b96d0","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-inversion"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Move **only have** in this perfect construction.","Move the first auxiliary **have** before they; keep the participle after the subject.","Choose **have they prepared**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Has does not agree with they.","B":"Move the first auxiliary **have** before they; keep the participle after the subject.","C":"This is ordinary statement order rather than the requested inversion.","D":"Only the first auxiliary moves; the participle stays after they."}}},{"id":"dc8a827d-1752-58e9-a52e-b41cb8d530ed","topicSlug":"inversion","level":"C1","prompt":"Replace “If we had prepared the evening meal”: “____ the evening meal, we would have finished earlier.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Had we prepared"},{"id":"B","text":"If had we prepared"},{"id":"C","text":"We had prepared"},{"id":"D","text":"Had we preparing"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"A formal unreal past condition can begin **Had + subject + participle**.","explanation":"Omit if and invert **had we** to express the unreal past condition.","keywords":["inverted-past-condition"],"skill":"inverted-past-condition","contextKey":"inversion-49","fingerprint":"895b18f0dd61b2c440cf83b6795f525da8ee0e564e3e68c85b796c010ab85eb7","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-inversion"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A formal unreal past condition can begin **Had + subject + participle**.","Omit if and invert **had we** to express the unreal past condition.","Choose **Had we prepared**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Omit if and invert **had we** to express the unreal past condition.","B":"Do not combine if with this inverted conditional structure.","C":"Without if or inversion, this would not mark the requested conditional relationship.","D":"Had requires a past participle, not -ing."}}},{"id":"857ee909-17e4-588b-a714-012a19d401b7","topicSlug":"inversion","level":"C1","prompt":"Use inversion in the main clause: “Only after the call ____ the evening meal.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"she did prepare"},{"id":"B","text":"did she prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"she prepared"},{"id":"D","text":"did she prepared"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"The inversion belongs in the **main clause following only after**.","explanation":"Only after the call is a limiting opening; invert **did she** and use base prepare.","keywords":["only-after-scope"],"skill":"only-after-scope","contextKey":"inversion-49","fingerprint":"d89ea81b0dff30753ce4f1552f4482e25a93586f2d325cd2a7fbaaaa8ff18fcf","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-inversion"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The inversion belongs in the **main clause following only after**.","Only after the call is a limiting opening; invert **did she** and use base prepare.","Choose **did she prepare**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The auxiliary must precede the subject in the inverted main clause.","B":"Only after the call is a limiting opening; invert **did she** and use base prepare.","C":"The requested formal main clause needs inversion after this limiting opening.","D":"Did already marks the past, so the next verb must be base."}}},{"id":"8602d316-7a0c-529e-acd0-fdc5dbc3615e","topicSlug":"inversion","level":"C1","prompt":"Use formal inversion: “Rarely ____ the rowing boat without help.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"she repairs"},{"id":"B","text":"does she repairs"},{"id":"C","text":"she does repair"},{"id":"D","text":"does she repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"A fronted negative-frequency expression triggers **auxiliary before subject**.","explanation":"Fronted **rarely** triggers **does she + repair**; does carries present agreement.","keywords":["negative-fronting-present"],"skill":"negative-fronting-present","contextKey":"inversion-50","fingerprint":"d444e6523b0d803f4d034d349bb06e55857de8f9377a8f3bd457e7e9b3f9a300","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-inversion"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A fronted negative-frequency expression triggers **auxiliary before subject**.","Fronted **rarely** triggers **does she + repair**; does carries present agreement.","Choose **does she repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"This retains ordinary statement order instead of the requested inversion.","B":"Does carries agreement, so the main verb must lose -s.","C":"The auxiliary must precede the subject after fronted rarely.","D":"Fronted **rarely** triggers **does she + repair**; does carries present agreement."}}},{"id":"dc953cd6-263b-58be-a455-bb8b57ca232e","topicSlug":"inversion","level":"C1","prompt":"Use formal inversion: “Never ____ the rowing boat so quickly.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"have repaired they"},{"id":"B","text":"has they repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"have they repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"they have repaired"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Move **only have** in this perfect construction.","explanation":"Move the first auxiliary **have** before they; keep the participle after the subject.","keywords":["negative-fronting-perfect"],"skill":"negative-fronting-perfect","contextKey":"inversion-50","fingerprint":"f6a7bb43aed94e9d86a7bea95385ccfc84fff90261ba98f2ca370d03b0fc271b","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-inversion"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Move **only have** in this perfect construction.","Move the first auxiliary **have** before they; keep the participle after the subject.","Choose **have they repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Only the first auxiliary moves; the participle stays after they.","B":"Has does not agree with they.","C":"Move the first auxiliary **have** before they; keep the participle after the subject.","D":"This is ordinary statement order rather than the requested inversion."}}},{"id":"282ac21f-54e6-558d-a5c5-1148396639bd","topicSlug":"inversion","level":"C1","prompt":"Replace “If we had repaired the rowing boat”: “____ the rowing boat, we would have finished earlier.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Had we repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"If had we repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"We had repaired"},{"id":"D","text":"Had we repairing"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"A formal unreal past condition can begin **Had + subject + participle**.","explanation":"Omit if and invert **had we** to express the unreal past condition.","keywords":["inverted-past-condition"],"skill":"inverted-past-condition","contextKey":"inversion-50","fingerprint":"be903847cf90692a35b16dfd71b44ac1ffbd2fa5ba7b4955367c0df361dfbf50","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-inversion"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A formal unreal past condition can begin **Had + subject + participle**.","Omit if and invert **had we** to express the unreal past condition.","Choose **Had we repaired**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Omit if and invert **had we** to express the unreal past condition.","B":"Do not combine if with this inverted conditional structure.","C":"Without if or inversion, this would not mark the requested conditional relationship.","D":"Had requires a past participle, not -ing."}}},{"id":"d960a41d-7e3c-5cd6-a614-78a811c8937f","topicSlug":"inversion","level":"C1","prompt":"Use inversion in the main clause: “Only after the call ____ the rowing boat.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"she repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"did she repaired"},{"id":"C","text":"she did repair"},{"id":"D","text":"did she repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The inversion belongs in the **main clause following only after**.","explanation":"Only after the call is a limiting opening; invert **did she** and use base repair.","keywords":["only-after-scope"],"skill":"only-after-scope","contextKey":"inversion-50","fingerprint":"b63f09738896b49ab17503e70ae554b647bfb648e2325a1ece919b6b64de5222","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-inversion"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The inversion belongs in the **main clause following only after**.","Only after the call is a limiting opening; invert **did she** and use base repair.","Choose **did she repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The requested formal main clause needs inversion after this limiting opening.","B":"Did already marks the past, so the next verb must be base.","C":"The auxiliary must precede the subject in the inverted main clause.","D":"Only after the call is a limiting opening; invert **did she** and use base repair."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Recognise formal emphasis that places an auxiliary before the subject, and conditional forms that omit if.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Negative or limiting openings","explanation":"After fronted negative or limiting expressions such as never, rarely and only then, formal English often uses **auxiliary–subject inversion** in the main clause. Add do-support if the original lexical verb has no auxiliary.","pattern":"**Rarely does she complain**.","commonMistakes":["Rarely she complains does not follow the standard emphatic inversion pattern after fronted rarely."],"examples":["Use formal inversion: “Rarely **does she prepare** the visitor guide without help.”","Use formal inversion: “Rarely **does she restore** the old portrait without help.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Move only the first auxiliary","explanation":"If a verb phrase already has auxiliaries, move just the first one before the subject. Keep later auxiliaries and the main verb after the subject. The form resembles a question, but the sentence is still a statement.","pattern":"**Never have I been** so surprised.","commonMistakes":["Never have been I ... moves too much of the verb phrase before the subject."],"examples":["Use formal inversion: “Never **have they prepared** the visitor guide so quickly.”","Use formal inversion: “Never **have they restored** the old portrait so quickly.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Conditional inversion without if","explanation":"Formal conditionals can omit if and begin with **had**, **were** or **should**. Had I known means if I had known. Were she to leave presents a hypothetical condition; should you need help presents a possible contingency.","pattern":"**Had I known**, I would have called.","commonMistakes":["Do not keep if as well as using this inverted conditional pattern."],"examples":["Replace “If we had prepared the visitor guide”: “**Had we prepared** the visitor guide, we would have finished earlier.”","Replace “If we had restored the old portrait”: “**Had we restored** the old portrait, we would have finished earlier.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Scope of only and not until","explanation":"An opening such as Only after the meeting or Not until she arrived triggers inversion in the **main clause**, not in the subordinate time clause. Only modifying the subject does not normally trigger this inversion.","pattern":"Only after she arrived **did we begin**.","commonMistakes":["Only the manager knew has no inversion because only modifies the subject itself."],"examples":["Use inversion in the main clause: “Only after the call **did she prepare** the visitor guide.”","Use inversion in the main clause: “Only after the call **did she restore** the old portrait.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**Rarely does she complain**.","**Never have I been** so surprised.","**Had I known**, I would have called.","Only after she arrived **did we begin**."]},"stage":"6 · Advanced control","prerequisites":["participle-clauses"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
